c4c18a8071085cb3bdcf9f7e68c3db00d930af9b0c7e1341fb3481"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65401" :question/id #uuid "be326033-9a6d-52c4-b689-ab656ecffefe" :question/math-academy-id "q-65401" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1+{tan}^{2}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1+{sec}^{2}⁡x-1\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression and use again $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1+{sec}^{2}⁡x-1 & ={sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+\\cancel{1}+{sec}^{2}⁡x-\\cancel{1} \\\\  & ={sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x-1) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x+1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65401/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65401"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3455"] :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3463"] :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} =  \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3144"] :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (1)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
